# Assignment 3: attempt-before-AI


## Statistics

| Item | Mean | Median | First-to-last change | Statistic I would report |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Staple A | 111.17 | 111.75 | +12.98% | Median (mean pulled down slightly by weak early weeks) |
| Oil B | 324.83 | 324.00 | +15.28% | Median (mean and median within 1 PKR; chosen for consistency) |
| Pulse C | 205.92 | 206.00 | +3.69% | Median (same reason) |
| Sweetener D | 166.75 | 166.75 | +19.41% | Median (identical to mean here; chosen for consistency) |

For "did it go up", the first-to-last change is the right statistic; the mean and median describe level, not movement.

## With and without a flagged value (stand-in)

Staple A with City 1 on 2026-01-08 changed from 99.5 to 995 (decimal slip): mean 111.17 -> 148.48 (+33.6%), median 111.75 -> 112.50 (+0.75). The mean uses the size of every value; the median uses only rank order.

## Draft note (O = observation, E = possible explanation, C = causal claim)

Referee's note: did the price "really" go up?

Between 1 Jan and 19 Mar 2026, the average price rose for all four items in the teaching data: Sweetener D by 19.4%, Oil B by 15.3%, Staple A by 13.0% and Pulse C by 3.7%. (O)
The mean and median over the whole period are almost identical for each item (for example Staple A: 111.17 vs 111.75), so the two neighbours are not disagreeing about the data. (O)
Both figures describe the typical level across the period, not whether the price moved, so neither settles the argument. (O)
A single wrong entry would split them: the median would barely move, but the mean can jump sharply. (O)
The steady weekly rise in Oil B and Sweetener D could reflect rising input costs, but the data contain nothing on costs. (E)
Pulse C's small rise could mean steadier supply or demand, which would need other data to check. (E)
We make no claim that any specific event or policy caused these movements. (O)
Verdict: use the first-to-last change for "did it go up" and the median for "what is a typical price"; use the mean only once the data have been checked for errors. (O)

Count: 6 O, 2 E, 0 C. No causal claim is made because the file holds only dates, items, cities and prices.
